%S A402187 1,4,2,12,4,24,2,32,8,32,2,144,2,32,32,160,4,256,8,384,16,64,4,768,64,
%T A402187 32,128,192,8,3072,4,768,16,64,128,6144,8,256,16,4096,4,9216,4,768,
%U A402187 1024,64,2,7680,4,2048,64,576,8,40960,192,1024,32,128,4,294912,8
%N A402187 Number of divisors of (5^n - 1)/4.
%C A402187 Let I[x] denote the set of polynomials p = p(x) all of whose coefficients are integers. For p(x) in I[x], denote by f(x,i), for i=1..m, the irreducible divisors in I[x] of p(x). Let c be an integer.  The numbers f(c,i), together with 1, are here called type 1 (p,c)-divisors of p(c).  The products of two or more of the numbers f(c,i) that are divisors of p(c) are type 2 (p,c)-divisors of p(c); and all other divisors of p(c) are type 3 (p,c)-divisors. These definitions generalize naturally to polynomials in more than 1 variable. (See A398834 for examples.)
%C A402187 For this sequence, the a(n) divisors of (5^n - 1)/4, for selected values of n are partitioned into the 3 types as shown below. Note that primes can occur in both types 1 or 3, or both, depending on n.
%C A402187    n    type 1           type 2          type 3
%C A402187    1    1                none            none
%C A402187    2    1,6              none            2,3
%C A402187    3    1,31             none            none
%C A402187    4    1,6,26           156             2,3,4,12,13,39,52,78
%C A402187    5    1,781            none            11,71
%C A402187 See A398834 for a guide to related sequences.
%t A402187 {x, y} = {5, 1}; p[n_] := p[n] = (x^n - y^n)/(x - y);
%t A402187 Table[DivisorSigma[0, p[n]], {n, 1, 70}]
%o A402187 (PARI) a(n) = numdiv((5^n - 1)/4); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Nov 19 2026
%Y A402187 Cf. A003463, A398834, A402188, A402189.
%K A402187 nonn,new
%O A402187 1,2
